---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Frequently asked questions"
  description="Answers about what Veasel Code can do today, model providers, local data, original brand artwork, and contributing."
>
  <section class="doc-page">
    <p class="kicker">FIELD GUIDE / COMMON QUESTIONS</p>
    <h1>
      Clear answers.
      <br />
      <em>Room to grow.</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Veasel Code is being built in public. Here is what the current source
      build does, what it does not do yet, and where to follow changes.
    </p>
    <div class="doc-panel faq-panel">
      <h2>Can Veasel Code change files in my repository?</h2>
      <p>
        It can propose a bounded edit to one text file at a time. In the TUI,
        inspect the saved diff with <code>/patch show &lt;id&gt;</code> and
        explicitly approve it with <code>/patch approve &lt;id&gt;</code>. The
        diff review starts with how many lines were added and removed, and a
        long diff keeps that summary on the first screen. A shell review shows
        whether a platform sandbox will wrap the command. The loopback API has
        no application token, so same-user processes can call its review and
        approval routes. Veasel can also propose shell commands; each one must
        be opened and approved with its exact command, working directory, and
        timeout. Approved commands may wrap with Bubblewrap, Seatbelt, or
        Windows AppContainer+Job when available — otherwise they fall through
        unsandboxed.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel faq-panel">
      <h2>Does my code stay on my machine?</h2>
      <p>
        Session history is stored in a local SQLite database managed by the
        Veasel Code backend. Prompts and recent conversation context are sent to
        the model provider you configure. If the model requests workspace tools,
        the matching file excerpts and tool results are sent to that provider
        too.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel faq-panel">
      <h2>How do I find a session when the home list is long?</h2>
      <p>
        The home sidebar keeps the selected session on screen. Press{" "}
        <kbd>j</kbd> / <kbd>k</kbd> or arrows to move, <kbd>g</kbd> /{" "}
        <kbd>G</kbd> for first/last, <kbd>1</kbd>–<kbd>9</kbd> to jump to a
        labeled slot, or type to filter by title or directory (<kbd>Ctrl+L</kbd>{" "}
        clears). Enter opens the selection. When the list is taller than the
        terminal, Veasel shows a position such as <code>8/20</code> and omits
        sessions outside that window. Long titles are clipped from the front and
        long directories from the end, so the workspace name stays visible.
        Press <kbd>n</kbd> to start a session when the list is empty. See the{" "}
        <a href={`${basePath}/docs/tui/`}>TUI guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel faq-panel">
      <h2>What happens if an async turn is interrupted?</h2>
      <p>
        Async session turns have a durable operation ID and status you can query
        after reconnecting. Queued <code>Prefer: respond-async</code> turns
        resume after restart (at most 32 queued per session) when the request
        still validates. Autonomous queued rows need{" "}
        <code>VEASEL_ALLOW_AUTONOMOUS_EXECUTION=1</code> or they cancel without
        starting. If the server stops after a worker claimed the turn but before
        storing a result, Veasel marks it <code>uncertain</code> and never
        retries a provider call or possible tool effect automatically. Inspect
        your workspace and operation status before deciding whether to try
        again.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel faq-panel">
      <h2>Which AI providers can I use?</h2>
      <p>
        The V backend supports OpenAI Chat Completions and Responses, Meta Model
        API Responses, Anthropic Messages, Gemini, and many OpenAI-compatible
        endpoints. These are four protocol families, not independent native
        adapters for every preset. See{" "}
        <a href={`${basePath}/docs/providers/`}>provider setup</a> and data
        disclosure details.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel faq-panel">
      <h2>How much text can one message contain?</h2>
      <p>
        A submitted conversation is limited to 1 MiB (1,048,576 bytes) of text.
        That limit is not a model's token window. Veasel does not yet use Muse's
        advertised 1,048,576-token context window. The output-token budget is
        filled in when you choose a model; press <kbd>Ctrl+O</kbd> to change it.
        Zero keeps Veasel's 4,096-token default, except Meta, which leaves the
        field unset so Meta applies its own limit.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel faq-panel">
      <h2>Can I use Agent Plugins already?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes for local packages. Veasel discovers directories (including durable
        operator-managed links via <code>/plugin add [--replace]</code> with
        install summaries), lets you enable validated Skill instructions per
        session, toggle package components, and expose trusted stdio or
        Streamable HTTP MCP servers as model tools. For a standalone server
        without a plugin package, use <code>/mcp add [user|project]</code>{" "}
        (user: <code>$VEASEL_DATA_DIR/mcp-servers.json</code>; project:{" "}
        <code>.veasel/mcp-servers.json</code>) or drop a portable{" "}
        <code>.mcp.json</code>, then <code>/mcp trust configured/name</code>.
        Named credentials and OAuth PKCE use <code>/mcp credentials</code> /{" "}
        <code>veasel mcp oauth</code> (see the{" "}
        <a href={`${basePath}/docs/mcp/`}>MCP guide</a>). Plugin servers still
        use <code>/mcp trust plugin/server</code>. Trust permits a server to
        connect; each model-requested tool call pauses the turn until you
        inspect its exact arguments with <code>/mcp calls</code> and approve or
        reject it. In the current source, that review says the call has not
        reached its server before the arguments, and a long argument list keeps
        the decision on the first screen. Rejected calls are not retried.
        Trusted stdio processes default to the server user's permissions; set{" "}
        <code>VEASEL_MCP_STDIO_SANDBOX=auto|require</code> for
        Linux/macOS/Windows wraps when available. Veasel does not fetch remote
        plugin packages or claim a marketplace.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel faq-panel">
      <h2>Does MCP OAuth open my browser automatically?</h2>
      <p>
        No. <code>veasel mcp oauth authorize … --listen</code> starts a loopback
        callback listener and prints the authorize URL to stderr. You open that
        URL yourself; Veasel does not launch a browser. After the redirect,
        complete the flow with the printed instructions (or{" "}
        <code>veasel mcp oauth complete</code> when you already have a code).
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel faq-panel">
      <h2>What is Harbor support?</h2>
      <p>
        Veasel ships a <strong>local</strong> Harbor installed-agent adapter
        pinned to Harbor <code>0.24.0</code>. Inside the task environment it
        installs Bubblewrap plus the verified Linux x86_64 release (SHA-256
        checked), then runs <code>veasel run</code> with an isolated data
        directory, loopback API, and the autonomous opt-in. Supported Harbor
        providers map to Veasel as <code>openai</code>,{" "}
        <code>openai-responses</code>, <code>anthropic</code>,{" "}
        <code>google</code>/<code>gemini</code>, <code>openai-compatible</code>,
        and Meta Model API as <code>meta</code>. When{" "}
        <code>veasel run --json</code> reports <code>usage_reported</code>,
        those counters are copied into Harbor agent metadata; cost stays
        unavailable without a price schedule. Veasel is not in Harbor’s built-in
        registry and this site does not claim Index or leaderboard placement.
        Details:{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/veasel-labs/veasel/blob/main/docs/compatibility/harbor.md">
          docs/compatibility/harbor.md
        </a>
        . Startup microbenchmark honesty lives on the{" "}
        <a href={`${basePath}/benchmarks/`}>benchmarks</a> page — including
        Linux <code>veasel-tui</code> version/help/PTY medians. This site does
        not claim Harbor Index placement or Windows ConPTY interactive smoke.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel faq-panel">
      <h2>Why is the terminal client SolidJS + OpenTUI?</h2>
      <p>
        The product TUI is a SolidJS application on OpenTUI (
        <code>@opentui/solid</code> + <code>@opentui/core</code>) with Veasel
        mint chrome, pixel splash, Compose, <kbd>Ctrl+P</kbd> command palette,
        review overlays, and <code>/</code> · <code>@</code> · <code>$</code>{" "}
        autocomplete. It talks to a versioned loopback API. The server, agent
        runtime, and persistence stay in V; the client remains a separate API
        consumer. Surfaces also include MarkdownRenderable for the latest live
        and completed turn, Diff content washes, context footers, system/tool
        theme tokens, custom JSON themes, and reduced-motion mode — see the{" "}
        <a href={`${basePath}/docs/tui/`}>TUI guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel faq-panel">
      <h2>Can I use the Veasel Code pixel companion?</h2>
      <p>
        The original mustelid pixel companion is included under this site's MIT
        license. The V language project's official mascot art is CC BY-NC 4.0
        and is not included or adapted here.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel faq-panel">
      <h2>How do I follow the project or contribute?</h2>
      <p>
        Read the <a href={`${basePath}/docs/roadmap/`}>verified roadmap</a>,
        open the repository, and use its issue or pull request templates to
        propose focused work.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="doc-next">
      <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/veasel-labs/veasel">
        Open Veasel Code on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${basePath}/docs/`}>
        Back to the field guide <span aria-hidden="true">↑</span>
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
